# Machine-local Kopia ignore rules -- gitignore syntax, anchored to $HOME.
#
# Copied to ~/.kopiaignore.local by install.sh and never tracked in git.
# The dotfiles repo is public; this file is where anything machine-specific
# goes, so the public kopiaignore stays free of identifying detail.
#
# Put here anything that would reveal:
#   - which applications are installed (app cache directories)
#   - client, employer or project names (directories under ~/projects)
#   - hostnames, usernames, bucket names
#
# A leading "/" anchors to $HOME. A trailing "/" matches directories only.
# Re-run `kopia snapshot estimate ~` after every change.

# --- Electron / chat / browser app caches ------------------------------
# These dirs are large and mostly regenerable, but naming them discloses your
# installed software. Add the ones that apply, e.g.:
#   /Library/Application Support/<app>/Cache/
#   /Library/Application Support/<app>/Code Cache/
#   /Library/Application Support/<app>/GPUCache/
#
# CAUTION: some app data under Application Support is device-only and cannot
# be recovered from anywhere else -- messaging history is the common case.
# Ignore specific cache subdirectories, not a whole app directory, unless you
# are certain the app's state is recoverable from its server.

# --- language toolchains not covered by the public list ----------------
#   /.<toolchain>/

# --- tool state and logs you consider disposable -----------------------
#   /.<tool>/projects/
#   /.<tool>/logs/

# --- large regenerable data under ~/projects ---------------------------
# Prefer a .gitignore inside the project (honoured automatically) over an
# entry here, so the rule travels with the repository.
#   /projects/<name>/<large-generated-dir>/

# vim: set ft=gitignore:
